  {
    "claim": "The paper argues that unconditioned language dissimilarity between groups is an unreliable indicator of polarization because groups naturally develop distinct lexicons, and that conditioning on a shared topic is necessary to isolate genuine partisan disagreement.",
    "quotes": [
      {"text": "the language of political discussions across socio-demographic groups naturally varies, with each group having their own lexicon. Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized positions.", "page": 4},
      {"text": "when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan groups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize correlates strongly with polarization.", "page": 4}
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim": "The paper asks what explains the cross-national difference in how online partisan polarization translates into public health outcomes, noting that the correlation between vaccine polarization and vaccination rates is strong in the US but notably weaker in Canada.",
    "quotes": [
      {"text": "This relationship was notably weaker in Canada, pointing to important cross-national differences in how political polarization manifests and impacts health behaviors.", "page": 1},
      {"text": "We did not observe a similar pattern in Canada, due to small sample size and the implementation of vaccine mandates.", "page": 16}
    ]
  }
